\details{
This function computes quantile shift effect sizes for all pairwise comparisons
in a two-way factorial design. Quantile shift measures distributional differences,
ranging from 0 to 1 with 0.5 indicating no effect.

Under normality and equal variances, Cohen's d values of 0, 0.2, 0.5, 0.8
correspond approximately to quantile shift values of 0.50, 0.55, 0.65, 0.70.

When \code{POOL=TRUE}, data are pooled across factor levels before computing
main effects. When \code{POOL=FALSE}, separate comparisons are made at each
level of the other factor.

This function tests all linear contrasts for main effects of Factors A and B
and all interactions using quantile shift methods.

\strong{Data Organization}:
Groups should be ordered as (1,1), (1,2), ..., (1,K), (2,1), ..., (J,K)
where the pair represents (Factor A level, Factor B level).

If groups are in the wrong order, use \code{grp} to rearrange them.
For example, for a 2×2 design, \code{grp=c(2,4,3,1)} indicates:
\itemize{
\item Group 2 corresponds to level (1,1)
\item Group 4 corresponds to level (1,2)
\item Group 3 corresponds to level (2,1)
\item Group 1 corresponds to level (2,2)
}

For an explanatory measure with pooling options, see \code{\link{bbmcpEP}}.

Missing values are automatically removed.
}
